+# RSpec Suite Optimization Notes
+
+## Baseline (2026-10-06, before models optimization)
+
+- Command: `bundle exec rspec spec/models --profile 20`
+- Result: **1795 examples, 0 failures, Finished in 2 minutes 42.5 seconds** (files took 2.62s to load)
+- Seed: 31864
+- Environment: Ruby 4.0.6, Rails test env, PostgreSQL 17, Redis 6379, no parallelization, CI=eager_load false locally.
+
+### Top 20 slowest examples (27.98s, 17.2% of total)
+- Trends::Statuses#refresh decays scores — 3.4s
+- Trends::Statuses#refresh returns correct statuses — 3.24s
+- MediaAttachment mp3 with large cover art — 2.63s
+- Trends::Statuses Query #filtered_for x4 — 1.28-1.63s each
+- AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy#statuses_to_delete x10 — ~0.9-1.18s each
+- Notification paginate/preload — ~1.0-1.08s each
+
+### Top slowest groups (avg per example)
+- Trends::Statuses: 1.12s avg (13.42s / 12 ex)
+- AccountSuggestions::FriendsOfFriendsSource: 0.49s avg
+- Status::ThreadingConcern: 0.44s avg (7.07s / 16 ex)
+- AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy: 0.43s avg (15.5s / 36 ex)
+- TagFeed: 0.388s avg (12.82s / 33 ex)
+- MediaAttachment: 0.266s avg (7.18s / 27 ex)
+- Notification: 0.31s avg (7.76s / 25 ex)
+
+### Per-file spot checks (without optimization)
+- spec/models/account_spec.rb (122 ex): ~3.98-4.32s
+- spec/models/status_spec.rb (62 ex): ~7.27-7.63s
+- spec/models/media_attachment_spec.rb (27 ex): ~6.95s
+- spec/models/user_spec.rb (72 ex): ~3.26s
+- spec/models/account_statuses_cleanup_policy_spec.rb (36 ex): ~8.6s
+- spec/models/tag_feed_spec.rb (33 ex): ~7.83s
+- spec/models/trends/statuses_spec.rb (12 ex): ~8.22s
+
+## Profiling method
+- Used `stackprof` gem (wall mode, interval 1000) via `--require /tmp/stackprof_helper.rb` on individual model files.
+- Example helper:
+  ```ruby
+  require 'stackprof'
+  StackProf.start(mode: :wall, out: '/tmp/stackprof.dump', interval: 1000)
+  RSpec.configure { |c| c.after(:suite) { StackProf.stop; StackProf.results('/tmp/stackprof.dump') } }
+  ```
+- Analyzed with `StackProf::Report`.
+
+## Key findings (shared bottlenecks)
+
+### 1. TriggerWebhookWorker inline execution dominates (~23-33% of wall time)
+- `rails_helper.rb` sets `Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)` globally before each test.
+- Every `Fabricate(:account)` → `Fabricate(:user)` triggers `User#trigger_webhooks` → `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async('account.created', ...)`.
+- Every `Fabricate(:status)` triggers `Status#trigger_create_webhooks` → `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async('status.created', ...)`.
+- With `:inline`, each `perform_async` immediately executes `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform` → `WebhookService#call` → `WebhookService#serialize_event` → ActiveModelSerializers + JSON.
+- StackProf for `tag_feed_spec.rb`: 23.2% in `Sidekiq::TestingClient#atomic_push` → `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform` (19.6% in AMS serialization).
+- For `account_statuses_cleanup_policy_spec.rb`: 27.7% in webhook path, 25.3% in `WebhookService#call`.
+- For `trends/statuses_spec.rb`: 33.7% in webhook path.
+- Note: `Status` fabricator also creates a `Bookmark` per status (after_create), whose own `account`+`user` cascade triggers additional `account.created` webhooks — amplifying the cost. Factories are out of scope for this phase, but stubbing webhooks mitigates the cascade cost.
+- Model specs that assert webhooks already stub `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async` with `allow(...).to receive` (e.g., `user_spec.rb`, `user/confirmation_spec.rb`, `follow_request_spec.rb`, `relay_spec.rb`), so global stub is safe. Specs asserting enqueues (`FASP`, `CacheBuster`, `Admin::AccountAction`) explicitly set `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)`, also safe.
+- No model spec was found to rely on inline webhook side effects (no `Webhook` records created in model specs that would need delivery; `webhook_spec.rb` only tests model validations/callbacks, not delivery).
+
+**Fix direction**: Default `type: :model` specs to `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` (or stub `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async`) with opt-in tag `sidekiq_inline: true` for specs needing inline. Clear `Sidekiq::Worker` jobs after each model test when using fake to avoid pollution.
+
+Spot-test with `allow(TriggerWebhookWorker).to receive(:perform_async)` for `type: :model`:
+- tag_feed: 7.83s → 5.27s (-32.6%)
+- cleanup_policy: 8.6s → 5.81s (-32%)
+- trends/statuses: 8.22s → 4.65s (-43%)
+- account: 4.32s → 3.39s (-21%)
+- status: 7.27s → 5.09s (-30%)
+- user: 3.26s → 2.81s (-14%)
+- media_attachment: 6.95s → 7.17s (no change, as expected — no webhooks)
+- rule: no change (light file)
+
+### 2. ActiveRecord logging overhead (~6-8%)
+- `ActiveRecord::LogSubscriber#sql` + `ActiveSupport::LogSubscriber#call` + `BroadcastLogger` visible in profiles (6-7% for AR SQL logging alone, plus AMS logging which disappears with webhook fix).
+- Test env uses default debug logging to `log/test.log`; every SQL + AMS render logs.
+- Potential: silence AR logger or raise log level for `type: :model` (verify no spec asserts on logs).
+
+### 3. Paperclip / media processing for non-media specs (~2% + FS I/O)
+- `Paperclip::Attachment#assign` + `post_process` ~2% in cleanup_policy (1 media per test). Heavier in `status_spec.rb` ordered_attachments (4 medias) and `avatar`/`header` specs.
+- `media_attachment_spec.rb` needs real processing (tests metadata, transcoding, blurhash, ffmpeg). Other model specs use media attachments only as associated records (e.g., `status_with_media` in cleanup policy) and do not assert on processed file metadata.
+- Potential: stub Paperclip processing globally for models except tagged specs (e.g., `:attachment_processing`).
+
+### 4. Minor / investigated but low value
+- `redis.del(redis.keys)` + `Rails.cache.clear` after each test: benchmarked <1ms per test (<2s total for 1795 tests). `flushdb` vs `del(keys)` similar for small key counts; not a major win. Still worth using `flushdb` for correctness/speed.
+- `Rails.application.reload_routes_unless_loaded` per test: cheap (`execute_unless_loaded`).
+- `allow(Resolv::DNS).to receive(:open)` per test: cheap.
+- `Faker` (~2-3%): `Faker::Base.translate` etc. in account/user/tag fabricators. Small; optimizing would touch factory behavior — deferred to factories phase.
+- `Chewy.strategy(:bypass)` already set; Chewy overhead ~1%.
+- `Mastodon::Snowflake` ~1%.
+- `Fabricate(:status)` → `Fabricate(:bookmark)` cascade + `Fabricate(:user)` → 2x `login_activity` cascade: major DB cost (74% in `Fabricate.create`), but explicitly out of scope for models phase (“do not touch factories yet”). Deferred to factories phase. Webhook stub already mitigates part of cascade cost.
+- `Bootsnap` 6% is load-time only.
+
+## Plan for models phase (no factory edits, no other spec types)
+1. Change `spec/rails_helper.rb`:
+   - Default `Sidekiq.testing!` to `:fake` for `type: :model` unless `sidekiq_inline: true` tag; keep `:inline` for other types.
+   - Stub `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async` for models (belt-and-braces, avoids even fake enqueue cost; still allows `have_received` assertions where specs already stub).
+   - Clear Sidekiq jobs after each model test.
+   - Optionally silence AR logging for models and stub Paperclip for non-media models with opt-in tag.
+2. Verify: run representative model files + full `spec/models` once; ensure 0 failures and ≥30-35% speedup.
+3. Keep changes minimal per-example: only add tags where needed for inline/paperclip.
+
+## Future (requests phase + factories phase)
+- Factories: remove `status → bookmark` after_create, reduce `user → 2x login_activity`, consider `build` vs `create` where associations not needed, optimize `Faker` usage.
+- Requests: re-evaluate Sidekiq inline needs (requests likely need inline for some flows); consider similar tagging.
+- Media: evaluate `Paperclip` stub strategy for requests as well.
+
+## Optimization applied (models phase, 2026-10-06)
+
+### Changes (4 files, no factories, no app code, no other spec types)
+
+1. **`spec/rails_helper.rb`** (config-level, scoped to `type: :model`):
+   - `Sidekiq.testing!`: `:fake` for models by default, `:inline` otherwise. Opt-in inline via `sidekiq_inline: true` tag.
+     ```ruby
+     config.before do |example|
+       if example.metadata[:type] == :model && !example.metadata[:sidekiq_inline]
+         Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)
+       else
+         Sidekiq.testing!(:inline)
+       end
+     end
+     ```
+   - Stub `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async` for models (avoids even fake-enqueue JSON + Queues overhead; still allows `have_received` in specs that explicitly stub):
+     ```ruby
+     config.before(:each, type: :model) do |example|
+       allow(TriggerWebhookWorker).to receive(:perform_async) unless example.metadata[:sidekiq_inline]
+     end
+     config.after(:each, type: :model) { Sidekiq::Worker.clear_all }
+     ```
+   - Silence `ActiveRecord::Base.logger` (set to `nil` during example, restore after) for models. Leaves `Rails.logger` intact. Opt-out via `enable_logging: true`.
+   - `redis.flushdb` instead of `redis.del(redis.keys)` for models (equivalent on dedicated test DB, faster/atomic).
+
+2. **Tags added** (only to preserve correctness for email specs that rely on Sidekiq inline via ActiveJob `:sidekiq` adapter + `capture_emails`):
+   - `spec/models/user_spec.rb`: `describe '#mark_email_as_confirmed!', sidekiq_inline: true`
+   - `spec/models/email_subscription_spec.rb`: `it 'generates token...', sidekiq_inline: true`
+   - `spec/models/admin/account_action_spec.rb`: `it 'sends email...', sidekiq_inline: true`
+   - No assertions changed. These 3 specs use `deliver_later` (Sidekiq) + `capture_emails` which requires inline (since `perform_enqueued_jobs` is no-op with `:sidekiq` adapter).
+
+### Why this is safe
+- Model specs asserting webhooks already stub `TriggerWebhookWorker` (`user_spec#approve!`, `user/confirmation_spec`, `follow_request`, `relay`); global stub is compatible (double `allow` before example, `have_received(...).with(...)` still checks matching args).
+- No model spec asserts `enqueue_sidekiq_job(TriggerWebhookWorker)`; FASP/CacheBuster/Suspension specs explicitly use `:fake` and assert other workers — still pass with global fake.
+- `ActiveRecord::Base.logger = nil` does not affect `Rails.logger` (separate reference after assignment); only `remotable_spec` asserts on `Rails.logger.debug` and still passes.
+- `flushdb` vs `del(keys)`: same effect on isolated test DB (DB 1).
+- Other spec types (`type != :model`) keep exact previous behavior (`:inline`, `del(keys)`, logging).
+
+### Results (after)
+- Command: `bundle exec rspec spec/models` (same env)
+- Run 1: **1795 examples, 0 failures, Finished in 58.9s** (seed 60129)
+- Run 2: **1795 examples, 0 failures, Finished in 57.97s** (seed 24293)
+- Baseline: 162.5s → **~58s, -64% time, 2.8x speedup** (target was -30-35%).
+- Per-group improvements (from `--profile 20`):
+  - Trends::Statuses: 13.42s → 3.49s (-74%, 1.12s → 0.29s avg)
+  - AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy: 15.5s → 4.53s (-71%)
+  - TagFeed: 12.82s → 4.38s (-66%)
+  - Status::ThreadingConcern: 7.07s → 2.18s (-69%)
+  - Notification: 7.76s → 1.97s (-75%)
+  - Status: ~7.6s → 4.23s (-44%)
+  - Account: ~4.3s → 2.9s (-32%)
+  - MediaAttachment: 7.18s → 6.75s (-6%, as expected — no webhooks; now the dominant group, needs real Paperclip/ffmpeg)
+- Spot checks all 0 failures, including FASP, follow_request, relay, confirmation, remotable, webhook, user, email_subscription, account_action.
+
+### Investigated but NOT applied (low value / out of scope)
+- Paperclip `post_process` stub for non-media models: ~2% in profiles, spot-test showed no measurable gain (VIPS in-process is fast for `attachment.jpg`; only media specs need real processing). Skipped to minimize risk.
+- `Faker` optimization: 2-3% but touches factory behavior — deferred to factories phase.
+- `Fabricate(:status)` → `bookmark` + `Fabricate(:user)` → 2x `login_activity` cascades: major DB cost (74% in `Fabricate.create`) but explicitly out of scope (“do not touch factories yet”). Webhook stub already mitigates cascade’s webhook part. Revisit in factories phase.
+- Global `ActiveJob` adapter switch to `:test` for models: would fix email specs without tags, but broader behavior change; tagging 3 specs is more minimal and preserves Sidekiq semantics.
+
+### Tags for future runs
+- `sidekiq_inline: true` — use for any model spec that needs real Sidekiq inline (e.g., `capture_emails` with `deliver_later` via `:sidekiq` adapter, or assertions on worker side effects like Redis merges). Currently used in 3 email specs.
+- `enable_logging: true` — opt out of AR log silencing if a future model spec needs to assert on SQL logs (none currently).
+- Full `spec/models` is now ~58s (was 162s). Combined `models + requests` 2x speedup goal is well underway; requests phase should re-evaluate Sidekiq needs (requests likely need inline for more flows) and consider similar tagging rather than blanket fake.
+
